# UTIME(2)

## NAME

utime - set file times

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <unistd.h>
int utime(const char *file, const time_t timep[2]);
```

## DESCRIPTION

The *utime* call uses the `accessed` and `updated` times in that order from the *timep* vector
to set the corresponding recorded times for *file.*

The caller must be the owner of the file or the super-user. The `inode-changed` time of the
file is set to the current time.

## SEE ALSO

stat (2)

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_utime** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
